## Migration: EventProcessor (.NET Framework → .NET 8)

### Key Problems in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — removed in .NET 5+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but unreliable; throws `ThreadInterruptedException` only from specific wait states |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` | Works, but is a hand-rolled producer/consumer that `Channel<T>` replaces entirely |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose items — only one `Dequeue` per signal |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _runTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        // ReadAllAsync drains the channel and blocks cooperatively
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                      // cancel if blocked waiting
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask.ConfigureAwait(false); // wait for clean exit
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) => Console.WriteLine(item);
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` | `Channel<string>` | Thread-safe, backpressure-aware, zero manual synchronization |
| `new Thread(RunLoop)` | `Task.Run(RunLoopAsync)` | Uses the thread pool; supports `async`/`await` |
| `Thread.Abort()` / `Interrupt()` | `CancellationToken` | Cooperative cancellation — the only supported pattern on .NET 5+ |
| No cleanup | `IAsyncDisposable` | Structured shutdown with `await using` |

### Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — this is the #1 migration blocker. Replace every call with `CancellationToken` cooperation.

2. **Lost-item race condition** — the original code signals once per enqueue but only dequeues one item per signal. If two items arrive before `WaitOne` returns, one is stuck until the *next* signal. `Channel.ReadAllAsync` eliminates this entirely.

3. **Exception handling** — `Thread.Abort` threw `ThreadAbortException` that auto-rethrew. Cancellation throws `OperationCanceledException`, which does **not** auto-rethrow. Catch it in the loop if you need cleanup:
   ```csharp
   try { await foreach (...) { } }
   catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* clean shutdown */ }
   ```

4. **Synchronous callers** — if `Stop()` must stay synchronous, use:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop()
   {
       _channel.Writer.Complete();
       _cts?.Cancel();
       _runTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult(); // blocks; avoid in UI/ASP.NET
   }
   ```

5. **Bounded channels** — for backpressure, switch to `CreateBounded<string>(capacity)` and use `await _channel.Writer.WriteAsync(item)` instead of `TryWrite`.
